</div>

<div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-lg-8">
        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-header-primary">
                <i class="bi bi-lightbulb"></i>
                The Problem This Solves
            </div>
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <p>
                    Structural steel fabrication projects generate complex
                    cutting lists — dozens of profiles, hundreds of parts,
                    each with specific lengths and quantities. Allocating
                    these manually to stock lengths wastes time and material.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This application automates the entire process — from
                    raw PDF to workshop-ready cutting list — in three clicks.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-header-primary">
                <i class="bi bi-diagram-3"></i>
                The Algorithm
            </div>
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <p>
                    The bin allocator uses a
                    <strong>First Fit Decreasing</strong> strategy —
                    a classic bin-packing heuristic. Parts are grouped
                    by profile then sorted by total meters descending.
                    Each bin is filled greedily until capacity is reached.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A 6m bin is chosen when remaining material fits within
                    6m. Otherwise a 13m bin is used. This minimises the
                    number of bins opened and reduces off-cut waste.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

